    "summary": "Soaring vocals, monster Truckin' > Spanish Jam, tight Dark Star",
    "blurb": "The Spectrum show delivers a powerhouse second set with an epic Truckin' into a soaring Spanish Jam and a crisp Dark Star. Jerry’s vocals are among his best, Keith’s organ crushes the jams and Bill locks in perfect time.",
    "review": "On March 24, 1973 at the Philadelphia Spectrum the Dead turned in one of their early‑’73 high points. The first set rolls through classic Americana—Bertha, Box of Rain, Here Comes Sunshine—and builds a solid groove, but it’s the second set that cements this night as legendary. A blistering “He’s Gone” launches into an astonishing 30‑minute “Truckin' > Spanish Jam” that feels like a single, soaring organism; Keith Godchaux’s organ fills are described as \"crushing\" and \"pure psychedelic jazz,\" trading riffs with Garcia whose guitar wails on Dark Star and rides the wave of his vocal performance—many reviewers call his singing “among his best.” The suite then slides into a tight Dark Star, followed by an emotional “Sing Me Back Home” that caps the set before the band rolls out a joyous “Sugar Magnolia” and a raucous “Johnny B. Goode” encore. Across all five soundboard sources the mix is crystal‑clear—especially the Betty board remastered by Jay Ashley—so every nuance, from Phil’s bomb‑dropping bass to Bill Kreutzmann’s drumming that sounds \"like an entire orchestra,\" shines through. This show is a must‑listen for anyone who loves the Dead at their improvisational peak.",
